    "summary": "High‑energy ’72 Academy set with stellar Looks Like Rain and epic jams",
    "blurb": "The show bursts open with a sublime pedal‑steel version of “Looks Like Rain” and tight rhythm work throughout. Standout moments include an extended “Playin’ In The Band” jam, the soaring “The Other One > Wharf Rat”, and a fiery Pigpen‑driven early set. Overall playing is inspired and the soundboard sources capture most of the magic.",
    "review": "March 21, 1972 at New York’s Academy of Music finds the Dead in top form, delivering a night that still feels fresh after five decades. The first set kicks off with “Bertha” and quickly moves into a breathtaking “Looks Like Rain”, where Jerry’s pedal‑steel floats over Phil’s booming bass and Pigpen’s soulful vocals. Highlights like “Greatest Story Ever Told” showcase Garcia’s wah‑inflected guitar, while the band rolls through “Playing In The Band” into an expansive jam that sets the tone for the second set. There, a 17‑minute “The Other One” morphs seamlessly into “Wharf Rat”, delivering one of the era’s most intense improvisational stretches. Pigpen shines again on “Ramblin’ Rose” and “Big Railroad Blues”, and the show closes with an exuberant “One More Saturday Night”. Across all five soundboard recordings the performance is consistently high‑energy, and the Miller SBD (gd1972-03-21.sbd.miller.92395.sbeok.flac16) offers the most complete, clear capture of this legendary night.",
